\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=702,\\,l=13,\\,w=6\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}702 & =13\\cdot 6\\cdot h \\\\ 702 & =78h \\\\ h & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ccb9c29a-bedd-593c-bf04-2353d6edadfb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccb9c29a-bedd-593c-bf04-2353d6edadfb" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb9c29a-bedd-593c-bf04-2353d6edadfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-425e9417-f634-5c8f-8996-3e931cdc3b7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d3784fb-09c4-56f5-bb03-0c4d51e5e003" "answer-1628a242-f264-5605-9435-46727417ea83" "answer-ce779af8-e3eb-5993-aabf-ae537338e442" "answer-432ba6f6-26b3-518f-871a-e1f91e9c7b54" "answer-425e9417-f634-5c8f-8996-3e931cdc3b7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d3784fb-09c4-56f5-bb03-0c4d51e5e003" :answer/id #uuid "5d3784fb-09c4-56f5-bb03-0c4d51e5e003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1628a242-f264-5605-9435-46727417ea83" :answer/id #uuid "1628a242-f264-5605-9435-46727417ea83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce779af8-e3eb-5993-aabf-ae537338e442" :answer/id #uuid "ce779af8-e3eb-5993-aabf-ae537338e442" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-432ba6f6-26b3-518f-871a-e1f91e9c7b54" :answer/id #uuid "432ba6f6-26b3-518f-871a-e1f91e9c7b54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-425e9417-f634-5c8f-8996-3e931cdc3b7c" :answer/id #uuid "425e9417-f634-5c8f-8996-3e931cdc3b7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120088 :question/problem "A bar of chocolate in the form of a rectangular prism is $9\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $2\\,\\text{cm}$ high. If the chocolate costs $$0.02$ per cubic centimeter, what is the value of four identical bars of chocolate?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of the bar of chocolate.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9,\\,w=5,\\,h=2\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=9\\cdot 5\\cdot 2=90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe total volume of the four bars of chocolate is\n\n$$\n90\\cdot 4=360\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the cost of four chocolate bars is\n\n$$\n360\\cdot 0.02=$7.20.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116734 :answer-field/correct 17592186116737 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-90edfdf5-24c3-54cd-8862-63c978f95f47" :question/id #uuid "90edfdf5-24c3-54cd-8862-63c978f95f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-118872" :question/problem "A person has seven identical boxes in the form of a rectangular solid. Each box is $10\\,\\text{in}$ long, $8\\,\\text{in}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{in}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the boxes?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of one box.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=10,\\,w=8,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=10\\cdot 8\\cdot 8=640\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the seven storage boxes is\n\n$$\n640\\cdot 7=4\\,480\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60d097f1-bd2b-5eac-9e0c-c6fa3b7f0885"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60d097f1-bd2b-5eac-9e0c-c6fa3b7f0885" :answer-field/id #uuid "60d097f1-bd2b-5eac-9e0c-c6fa3b7f0885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cb4cf501-4a8b-53d9-ba1c-97b7ebf0dc55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb4cf501-4a8b-53d9-ba1c-97b7ebf0dc55" "answer-2db94192-9608-59d6-be5f-b026801ae9ce" "answer-a7c962e9-6e8e-5b95-b1f4-d86b14823f96" "answer-04a735cc-2bb5-57f8-afb8-bf8a9f6cfe7b" "answer-253a500f-10a7-5a2b-9a1d-48a97e3d0acb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb4cf501-4a8b-53d9-ba1c-97b7ebf0dc55" :answer/id #uuid "cb4cf501-4a8b-53d9-ba1c-97b7ebf0dc55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,480\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2db94192-9608-59d6-be5f-b026801ae9ce" :answer/id #uuid "2db94192-9608-59d6-be5f-b026801ae9ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,040\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7c962e9-6e8e-5b95-b1f4-d86b14823f96" :answer/id #uuid "a7c962e9-6e8e-5b95-b1f4-d86b14823f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "640\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04a735cc-2bb5-57f8-afb8-bf8a9f6cfe7b" :answer/id #uuid "04a735cc-2bb5-57f8-afb8-bf8a9f6cfe7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,400\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-253a500f-10a7-5a2b-9a1d-48a97e3d0acb" :answer/id #uuid "253a500f-10a7-5a2b-9a1d-48a97e3d0acb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "720\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e195bf20-65ce-5bd5-85a8-c18879f5cecb" :question/id #uuid "e195bf20-65ce-5bd5-85a8-c18879f5cecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-118873" :question/problem "A shipping company delivered six identical packages in the form of a rectangular prism. Each package is $40\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $20\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the packages?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of one package.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=40,\\,w=20,\\,h=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=40\\cdot 20\\cdot 10=8\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the six packages is\n\n$$\n8\\,000\\cdot 6=48\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-187877f5-c38d-5e21-86e6-44ed4b5fbfcc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-187877f5-c38d-5e21-86e6-44ed4b5fbfcc" :answer-field/id #uuid "187877f5-c38d-5e21-86e6-44ed4b5fbfcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ca2f13e7-3f99-5d2e-8c4c-1840ccbbf980" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15b438ae-a475-5594-ad18-73d79bb97fd8" "answer-ca2f13e7-3f99-5d2e-8c4c-1840ccbbf980" "answer-4161ce64-ccaa-5a03-9428-ac5e7b833fc4" "answer-3aa5ef76-016b-5b8c-a1bf-7bbd48e16bf8" "answer-3c929f1b-223c-53e1-a752-772c1da2087e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15b438ae-a475-5594-ad18-73d79bb97fd8" :answer/id #uuid "15b438ae-a475-5594-ad18-73d79bb97fd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "38\\,400\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca2f13e7-3f99-5d2e-8c4c-1840ccbbf980" :answer/id #uuid "ca2f13e7-3f99-5d2e-8c4c-1840ccbbf980" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4161ce64-ccaa-5a03-9428-ac5e7b833fc4" :answer/id #uuid "4161ce64-ccaa-5a03-9428-ac5e7b833fc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aa5ef76-016b-5b8c-a1bf-7bbd48e16bf8" :answer/id #uuid "3aa5ef76-016b-5b8c-a1bf-7bbd48e16bf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,400\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c929f1b-223c-53e1-a752-772c1da2087e" :answer/id #uuid "3c929f1b-223c-53e1-a752-772c1da2087e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-014f97dd-12f1-54e9-8347-3c53a77026ab" :question/id #uuid "014f97dd-12f1-54e9-8347-3c53a77026ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-107731" :question/problem "![](images/62/6240abf268497f82475003619689d979b0b00678b4d49972ff2fbcf1fa866a9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a cubic gas tank. One-fifth of the volume of the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?" :question/worked-solution "The length of $7\\,\\text{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents one-fifth of the tank's side length. Therefore, the length of each side of the tank is\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 7=35\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the cube is a rectangular prism that is $35\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $35\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $35-7=28\\,\\text{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=28\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=35\\cdot 35\\cdot 28=34\\,300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to fill the tank to capacity, it would cost\n\n$$\n34\\,300\\cdot 0.0016=$54.88.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab997a9e-66ed-5632-8075-dc62b6beef15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab997a9e-66ed-5632-8075-dc62b6beef15" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab997a9e-66ed-5632-8075-dc62b6beef15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1925be10-596c-557e-b94e-7843f3e27ddf" "answer-2c6bbdb9-99c3-511d-98b5-292b6419acef" "answer-f17db65a-f656-5936-ad46-cb4b659e7dad" "answer-c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" "answer-b1a0135e-96f1-5953-b50e-81d6419a564b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1925be10-596c-557e-b94e-7843f3e27ddf" :answer/id #uuid "1925be10-596c-557e-b94e-7843f3e27ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$47.72$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c6bbdb9-99c3-511d-98b5-292b6419acef" :answer/id #uuid "2c6bbdb9-99c3-511d-98b5-292b6419acef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$49.95$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f17db65a-f656-5936-ad46-cb4b659e7dad" :answer/id #uuid "f17db65a-f656-5936-ad46-cb4b659e7dad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$50.25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" :answer/id #uuid "c0a2dbc0-3274-5cf1-8bab-4898a8a2fd18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$54.88$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1a0135e-96f1-5953-b50e-81d6419a564b" :answer/id #uuid "b1a0135e-96f1-5953-b50e-81d6419a564b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$57.42$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e2801a7-e084-5269-a941-751cceb06faf" :question/id #uuid "3e2801a7-e084-5269-a941-751cceb06faf" :question/math-academy-id "q-107630" :question/problem "A storage center has ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular solid. Each unit is $9\\,\\text{ft}$ long, $24\\,\\text{ft}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{ft}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the units?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9,\\,w=24,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=9\\cdot 24\\cdot 8=1\\,728\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the ten storage units is\n\n$$\n1\\,728\\cdot 10=17\\,280\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-47ed7cb6-b944-5862-8ee4-2c83a098bcf5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-47ed7cb6-b944-5862-8ee4-2c83a098bcf5" :answer-field/id #uuid "47ed7cb6-b944-5862-8ee4-2c83a098bcf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41a556a7-74b8-5f77-8473-47998a47590d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d197ef72-bbca-5188-bd5b-b8ee0acc0017" "answer-ccada324-ead9-51a5-afcc-0e4711ff3f76" "answer-41a556a7-74b8-5f77-8473-47998a47590d" "answer-d9117a9a-638d-54bf-ba34-918349539f35" "answer-c4120e78-7ffa-5428-886a-a8780dc4f9de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d197ef72-bbca-5188-bd5b-b8ee0acc0017" :answer/id #uuid "d197ef72-bbca-5188-bd5b-b8ee0acc0017" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,840\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccada324-ead9-51a5-afcc-0e4711ff3f76" :answer/id #uuid "ccada324-ead9-51a5-afcc-0e4711ff3f76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,800\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41a556a7-74b8-5f77-8473-47998a47590d" :answer/id #uuid "41a556a7-74b8-5f77-8473-47998a47590d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17\\,280\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9117a9a-638d-54bf-ba34-918349539f35" :answer/id #uuid "d9117a9a-638d-54bf-ba34-918349539f35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,480\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4120e78-7ffa-5428-886a-a8780dc4f9de" :answer/id #uuid "c4120e78-7ffa-5428-886a-a8780dc4f9de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19\\,760\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121085 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rectangular prism below. How can we calculate its volume?  \n\n![](images/94/94bdf8333e1cb6a7ff9f99bc4ef854433bc99a8310f61181309cf51709e7569c.png)\n\nIn general, we can find the volume $V$ of a rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV = lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nl = 20\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad w=10\\,\\textrm{cm}, \\qquad h=5\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =20\\cdot 10\\cdot 5=1\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119986 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186174676 :answer-field/choices 17592186174677] [:db/retract 17592186174676 :answer-field/choices 17592186174678] [:db/retract 17592186174676 :answer-field/choices 17592186174679] [:db/retract 17592186174676 :answer-field/choices 17592186174680] [:db/retract 17592186174676 :answer-field/choices 17592186174681] {:db/id 17592186175969 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" "question-b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" "question-b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" "question-5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175819 :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=2-y^2,$ $x=y,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown below. Using the shell method, find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/16/16a30b8310128aa588b3278b5a0984193ce57cbb5ea9118fc167a636487363b8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=2-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/f6/f69e2c1cd3b099a4ba192357db32274867c4f296e2c34e31e5110ee31b270317.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=2-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175968 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" "question-e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" "question-1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" "question-1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" "question-efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175818 :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=3y-y^2$ and $x=y,$  as shown below. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7e/7eca2a7eecfb9555a7e1555555a6d7b435a794e5715537d4fe94998ef00d124a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=3y-y^2$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/86/861a1300845e3832a201dd00410d462350f33a4654332b6b6fa7140dc6476dbb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=3y-y^2.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([3y-{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{16}{3}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" :question/id #uuid "1ede84af-72eb-557a-81fe-42dab11d2478" :question/math-academy-id "q-107218" :question/problem "![](images/89/8957a7db313379597a21e6b51d6c4493058af207a2d8edd5fafb8e549ba45aae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1+{y}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1+{y}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1+{y}^{3} \\\\ y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y(1-y)(1+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/b9/b91dedd1282ce1e9d8546bc6f7c52eba8741a2cd49613a03ef62866a9ba91b0c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1+{y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+y]-[1+{y}^{3}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48b6c840-af69-50a7-90ae-fcfa0390abc1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48b6c840-af69-50a7-90ae-fcfa0390abc1" :answer-field/id #uuid "48b6c840-af69-50a7-90ae-fcfa0390abc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73e752f4-3838-5b2c-ae9b-87e14a752cbf" "answer-80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" "answer-0a54eb67-bb5d-5e42-97b8-8eefbb20b3ec" "answer-e71d3b21-f148-548e-a6ef-acd9b0a583ab" "answer-006652b3-463c-58d3-a96f-cbc2e068d0ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73e752f4-3838-5b2c-ae9b-87e14a752cbf" :answer/id #uuid "73e752f4-3838-5b2c-ae9b-87e14a752cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" :answer/id #uuid "80a9bc50-5c65-5ff2-8cbc-ddd7ad27ca94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a54eb67-bb5d-5e42-97b8-8eefbb20b3ec" :answer/id #uuid "0a54eb67-bb5d-5e42-97b8-8eefbb20b3ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{3}-y)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e71d3b21-f148-548e-a6ef-acd9b0a583ab" :answer/id #uuid "e71d3b21-f148-548e-a6ef-acd9b0a583ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-006652b3-463c-58d3-a96f-cbc2e068d0ad" :answer/id #uuid "006652b3-463c-58d3-a96f-cbc2e068d0ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y({y}^{3}-2y)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" :question/id #uuid "b61e8b5a-f6d1-5f01-ad3a-3725586d9405" :question/math-academy-id "q-106122" :question/problem "![](images/30/30aa4c5e72919d09a0a2323dc8af90a73810047857f3c89fe9c3005a10c195ae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cb/cbfd6c409590e6b6d350b5ae8f6531944467e90991b17582febe228a6a8dfdb2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ba66103-f7c3-5909-8944-c611bc72d320"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ba66103-f7c3-5909-8944-c611bc72d320" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba66103-f7c3-5909-8944-c611bc72d320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" "answer-67cda087-075b-574c-aace-9ef713972155" "answer-a3066621-d9af-51db-9c80-ef7f8e379a36" "answer-15608928-4e7f-5b0d-a6ce-90c1e1a5b6a2" "answer-9dc07ce1-b348-5d8f-acd3-7b08c3d96c9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" :answer/id #uuid "b712480b-490e-5af1-9fec-c822b73444ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67cda087-075b-574c-aace-9ef713972155" :answer/id #uuid "67cda087-075b-574c-aace-9ef713972155" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y({y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3066621-d9af-51db-9c80-ef7f8e379a36" :answer/id #uuid "a3066621-d9af-51db-9c80-ef7f8e379a36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15608928-4e7f-5b0d-a6ce-90c1e1a5b6a2" :answer/id #uuid "15608928-4e7f-5b0d-a6ce-90c1e1a5b6a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2y-{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dc07ce1-b348-5d8f-acd3-7b08c3d96c9a" :answer/id #uuid "9dc07ce1-b348-5d8f-acd3-7b08c3d96c9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{({y}^{2}-4y)}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" :question/id #uuid "b0ffc976-87a0-51e6-8ff5-4a0a8b70546a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126325" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b61c628a012f6bde3144254b521e11f8803e089cd7f83f2c19a1255cf22c0e3a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},$ $x=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}-y+1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}{y}^{2}+y+3 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2}-4y-12 & =0 \\\\ (5y+6)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/12/122a21e72dc7473cc34ed3ece0325294eadcc86cbe8a5b91e4dfe4be77b369c0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}y+1])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0601c164-985b-5dd4-912d-b59e95bef8da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0601c164-985b-5dd4-912d-b59e95bef8da" :answer-field/id #uuid "0601c164-985b-5dd4-912d-b59e95bef8da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63c110bb-5316-5161-b14e-6b363f107559" "answer-7f54ff01-aed0-58e9-858f-625c9907586d" "answer-75eaed8c-a198-5c3a-b426-50603d367304" "answer-89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" "answer-b4882823-b7fc-57ca-8289-748a54539d36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63c110bb-5316-5161-b14e-6b363f107559" :answer/id #uuid "63c110bb-5316-5161-b14e-6b363f107559" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-y-\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f54ff01-aed0-58e9-858f-625c9907586d" :answer/id #uuid "7f54ff01-aed0-58e9-858f-625c9907586d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}y+1)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75eaed8c-a198-5c3a-b426-50603d367304" :answer/id #uuid "75eaed8c-a198-5c3a-b426-50603d367304" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(1-\\frac{1}{2}y-\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" :answer/id #uuid "89944f1d-1e72-5cb7-b5b1-8e207883c50a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}y-1)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4882823-b7fc-57ca-8289-748a54539d36" :answer/id #uuid "b4882823-b7fc-57ca-8289-748a54539d36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}+y-2)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9" :question/id #uuid "5dabe85e-85d9-5f83-8489-ec7933e9dac9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126191" :question/problem "![](images/da/dadb1e7740ee89fbbf9cffe69501ebd7be655ec9aaee30071b09fc767e80092c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+2y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-y)(1+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d9/d9f7cf87c59b7a0fc9600178795524f35a72b12466385e63eafa35c1fdb3537c.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+2y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1+2y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+2y-{y}^{2}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7ab163e-5d5d-5698-b7ab-03eea7c74f9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7ab163e-5d5d-5698-b7ab-03eea7c74f9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7ab163e-5d5d-5698-b7ab-03eea7c74f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc9016d5-be3d-5277-aaaf-8658739447a8" "answer-139fc7af-0867-5a86-940c-8281935a937b" "answer-6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" "answer-a5fa5ee1-55ae-551e-b55c-cb55db8dd6f5" "answer-2f7b25f4-6ce8-527a-8907-b4e941ca805d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc9016d5-be3d-5277-aaaf-8658739447a8" :answer/id #uuid "fc9016d5-be3d-5277-aaaf-8658739447a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1+4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-139fc7af-0867-5a86-940c-8281935a937b" :answer/id #uuid "139fc7af-0867-5a86-940c-8281935a937b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" :answer/id #uuid "6f3a483b-b14e-5eaf-a6fa-5d488f2042e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5fa5ee1-55ae-551e-b55c-cb55db8dd6f5" :answer/id #uuid "a5fa5ee1-55ae-551e-b55c-cb55db8dd6f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(1+{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f7b25f4-6ce8-527a-8907-b4e941ca805d" :answer/id #uuid "2f7b25f4-6ce8-527a-8907-b4e941ca805d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176403 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $x=y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 5y-2{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/d8/d873c0acb12392e4ea40f761c72c1903a91c4f0e9fef04b586a71697957e2401.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([5y-2{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174676 :answer-field/correct "answer-9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb5199d3-7358-5a1e-8adf-9222be3fa0d7" "answer-9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" "answer-1f4675ff-30c4-572a-8b6c-5272dc8a8aeb" "answer-ff744269-4ea5-571c-8ec4-ce039a7674e4" "answer-3383ee3a-fd4a-5273-a400-128ba9a72cfa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb5199d3-7358-5a1e-8adf-9222be3fa0d7" :answer/id #uuid "bb5199d3-7358-5a1e-8adf-9222be3fa0d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2{y}^{2}-4y)\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" :answer/id #uuid "9fc3c7d3-e229-5ae0-8d8b-3f3ed9e9f05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f4675ff-30c4-572a-8b6c-5272dc8a8aeb" :answer/id #uuid "1f4675ff-30c4-572a-8b6c-5272dc8a8aeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(6y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff744269-4ea5-571c-8ec4-ce039a7674e4" :answer/id #uuid "ff744269-4ea5-571c-8ec4-ce039a7674e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(4y-2{y}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3383ee3a-fd4a-5273-a400-128ba9a72cfa" :answer/id #uuid "3383ee3a-fd4a-5273-a400-128ba9a72cfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y{(2{y}^{2}-4y)}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" :question/id #uuid "f03e4c2d-a256-544b-8a7f-59a90059d73b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352372" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcb67e2d3fe9a413c02bb00b6d8cf7c0ac8825db1d360ab883f08baa3b6c2e9c.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=7-y-{y}^{2},$ $x=5-{y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Now, let $f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=5-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 7-y-{y}^{2} & =5-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2-y & =0 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/cf/cfb914bf7ed0a0c6b9f784d46a6761cd97c352fcc4e82abb29a72dc12d8ab7ca.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=5-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=7-y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([7-y-{y}^{2}]-[5-{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2(\\frac{4}{2}-0)-(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c63fd60-a918-5151-ad1a-213063aeef6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c63fd60-a918-5151-ad1a-213063aeef6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c63fd60-a918-5151-ad1a-213063aeef6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455" :answer/id #uuid "6a9bfb4c-8588-57d6-bd1d-a486a788d455" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" :question/id #uuid "e40037f0-3304-53a5-a4f4-7220f338664f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126377" :question/problem "![](images/48/482781bb21d011df5a9140b2b1fb0ebe1108f746c0055077d4f4e9a91fce735d.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ $x={y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)={y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/48/48905a174d688fdb3e099debb7840173a2ce047816003a5be9c089997196f9fb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=f(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0ac10175-16bb-5872-9c8f-0de12b255415"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0ac10175-16bb-5872-9c8f-0de12b255415" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac10175-16bb-5872-9c8f-0de12b255415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96c8069d-b163-5345-aa21-42e30dfc170b" "answer-0bdd21a9-b33a-5551-894f-eefc01cd484b" "answer-dd29541b-ac6a-5c52-b43d-f88aae4b76d5" "answer-54fb0fe4-d278-59fe-baf1-7676faf2662c" "answer-36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96c8069d-b163-5345-aa21-42e30dfc170b" :answer/id #uuid "96c8069d-b163-5345-aa21-42e30dfc170b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bdd21a9-b33a-5551-894f-eefc01cd484b" :answer/id #uuid "0bdd21a9-b33a-5551-894f-eefc01cd484b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd29541b-ac6a-5c52-b43d-f88aae4b76d5" :answer/id #uuid "dd29541b-ac6a-5c52-b43d-f88aae4b76d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54fb0fe4-d278-59fe-baf1-7676faf2662c" :answer/id #uuid "54fb0fe4-d278-59fe-baf1-7676faf2662c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8" :answer/id #uuid "36e02b44-a709-533e-8923-9cd6635295c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" :question/id #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107142" :question/problem "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" :answer-field/id #uuid "eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" "answer-b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" "answer-3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" "answer-19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" "answer-d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" :answer/id #uuid "0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" :answer/id #uuid "b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" :answer/id #uuid "3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644" :answer/id #uuid "d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" :question/id #uuid "1e2fc7b4-11f3-5f1c-807e-b40bd8df74a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107304" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5eac1b1d21378d64b90fc2463eb7664b30224594ba5d63aa70a7c412097012a0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(3-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/84/84e62b99598649ac48d55171b0d6bf7e5d26e38a6ad94c46ef9c89145e60e29a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y([1+y]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (3(\\frac{27}{3}-0)-(\\frac{81}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-410a3349-db09-5c6c-877b-7b43454d7322"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-410a3349-db09-5c6c-877b-7b43454d7322" :answer-field/id #uuid "410a3349-db09-5c6c-877b-7b43454d7322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aedf06dc-11bf-5210-87fb-2324f78a6574" "answer-1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" "answer-e847bcfd-c659-5ed7-a215-e4e98478c99c" "answer-9ee05a02-1be6-5002-8a40-86020d83d578" "answer-efd08d18-ffa2-5e0a-8532-bcddc6c9bf2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aedf06dc-11bf-5210-87fb-2324f78a6574" :answer/id #uuid "aedf06dc-11bf-5210-87fb-2324f78a6574" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" :answer/id #uuid "1853b3af-28f4-5ef9-aad8-03a6eaf50446" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e847bcfd-c659-5ed7-a215-e4e98478c99c" :answer/id #uuid "e847bcfd-c659-5ed7-a215-e4e98478c99c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ee05a02-1be6-5002-8a40-86020d83d578" :answer/id #uuid "9ee05a02-1be6-5002-8a40-86020d83d578" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efd08d18-ffa2-5e0a-8532-bcddc6c9bf2c" :answer/id #uuid "efd08d18-ffa2-5e0a-8532-bcddc6c9bf2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{81\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8" :question/id #uuid "efbcafae-ea63-52c2-855e-a9cfa22be6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352199" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d8d3dfa1134b7fd5861ff70e493ead2227dd2d322e8436e28a903e0c596a408.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 4y-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/c5/c5a117e2af85e3380942939812442eac7b899a2259af79ad032654fc5662a767.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (4(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{16}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}-8) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b70140cd-8dd2-5535-a3c9-379587f1fc13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b70140cd-8dd2-5535-a3c9-379587f1fc13" :answer-field/id #uuid "b70140cd-8dd2-5535-a3c9-379587f1fc13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa" :answer/id #uuid "7553f952-2b67-5919-b814-110becde50fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176983 :tutorial/content "Consider the region enclosed by the curves $x=f(y)$ on the right, $x=g(y)$ on the left, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d,$ as shown below. We assume that $f(y) \\geq g(y)$ for all $y \\in [c,d].$ \n\n![](images/9a/9a3970253f710223a77fe43f717df908fc20f27950744166ea7197897d64936d.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the shell method as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([f(y)]-[g(y)])\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176158 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157316 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157868 :question/problem "At a water bottle factory, every batch of $1\\,000$ water bottles has $20$ water bottles (on average) on which the label is upside down. If an inspector comes to inspect the water bottles, what is the probability that they will find an upside down label before checking the $5$th bottle? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of bottles checked until a defect is found. On average, the chance of finding an upside down label on any water bottle is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{20}{1\\,000}=0.02.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of bottles that the inspector checks before finding an upside down label can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.02.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.02).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.02)^{x-1}(0.02) \\\\  & =(0.98)^{x-1}(0.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding an upside down label before checking the $5$th bottle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.98)^{1-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{2-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{3-1}(0.02)+(0.98)^{4-1}(0.02) \\\\  & =(0.02)+(0.98)(0.02)+(0.98)^{2}(0.02)+(0.98)^{3}(0.02) \\\\  & =[1+0.98+(0.98)^{2}+(0.98)^{3}](0.02) \\\\  & ≈0.078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186156129 :answer-field/correct 17592186156132 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea" :question/id #uuid "adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-85881" :question/problem "An experiment consists of tossing a fair coin until it comes up heads. What is the probability that we get our first head on the $3$rd toss? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the coin is tossed until it comes up heads. Since the chance of getting a head in every toss is $50%,$ the number of tosses until it comes up heads can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=50%=\\frac{50}{100}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that head appears in the $3$rd toss is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}(0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02f2874f-c8cb-5424-9558-d25dbc1321ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02f2874f-c8cb-5424-9558-d25dbc1321ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "02f2874f-c8cb-5424-9558-d25dbc1321ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c19ac48b-f028-591b-b391-957b90b26cb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-38a40095-28ea-5b29-9f46-5d1722a2dc00" "answer-511ee1e7-4a2e-5ac2-b693-bbdf3c003f14" "answer-bd14f852-afc2-5508-a508-f915bcd1125c" "answer-c19ac48b-f028-591b-b391-957b90b26cb8" "answer-4ecdbb4d-cd29-5c6c-8d54-821afb7fd664"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38a40095-28ea-5b29-9f46-5d1722a2dc00" :answer/id #uuid "38a40095-28ea-5b29-9f46-5d1722a2dc00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.095" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-511ee1e7-4a2e-5ac2-b693-bbdf3c003f14" :answer/id #uuid "511ee1e7-4a2e-5ac2-b693-bbdf3c003f14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.098" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd14f852-afc2-5508-a508-f915bcd1125c" :answer/id #uuid "bd14f852-afc2-5508-a508-f915bcd1125c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c19ac48b-f028-591b-b391-957b90b26cb8" :answer/id #uuid "c19ac48b-f028-591b-b391-957b90b26cb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.125" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ecdbb4d-cd29-5c6c-8d54-821afb7fd664" :answer/id #uuid "4ecdbb4d-cd29-5c6c-8d54-821afb7fd664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.160" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02" :question/id #uuid "934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02" :question/math-academy-id "q-85889" :question/problem "A lottery decides that $3$ out of every $20$ lottery tickets will win a special prize. If a player wants to win a special prize, what is the probability of finding a prize before purchasing their $5$th ticket? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of tickets purchased until a special prize is found. On average, the chance of finding a special prize on a single ticket is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of tickets that the player should purchase before they find a special prize can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.15.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a special prize before the $5$th ticket is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{4-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}+(0.85)^{3}](0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.478\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d865ca2-a464-54ee-8571-e4cc8b40fa53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d865ca2-a464-54ee-8571-e4cc8b40fa53" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d865ca2-a464-54ee-8571-e4cc8b40fa53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e6c7f4a-9255-55cc-ab48-64524d9d60bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f594278-070e-5cca-b2b4-69634d02b41b" "answer-82348db0-740d-5037-8bfb-dd1638b0599c" "answer-2e6c7f4a-9255-55cc-ab48-64524d9d60bd" "answer-bb376c02-f849-5a37-bcfc-6f47602f5bc8" "answer-1ed681c4-3034-59aa-a498-ea702cbb65c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f594278-070e-5cca-b2b4-69634d02b41b" :answer/id #uuid "2f594278-070e-5cca-b2b4-69634d02b41b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.391" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82348db0-740d-5037-8bfb-dd1638b0599c" :answer/id #uuid "82348db0-740d-5037-8bfb-dd1638b0599c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.423" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e6c7f4a-9255-55cc-ab48-64524d9d60bd" :answer/id #uuid "2e6c7f4a-9255-55cc-ab48-64524d9d60bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.478" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb376c02-f849-5a37-bcfc-6f47602f5bc8" :answer/id #uuid "bb376c02-f849-5a37-bcfc-6f47602f5bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.501" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ed681c4-3034-59aa-a498-ea702cbb65c0" :answer/id #uuid "1ed681c4-3034-59aa-a498-ea702cbb65c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.512" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157317 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" "question-bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" :question/id #uuid "e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" :question/math-academy-id "q-85897" :question/problem "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?" :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e64d04fb-cb14-517f-be04-f2bf97158deb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e64d04fb-cb14-517f-be04-f2bf97158deb" :answer-field/id #uuid "e64d04fb-cb14-517f-be04-f2bf97158deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-923cac8c-34f5-5ea2-bb76-7bdba561dd9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-11d7504b-2828-5ed4-ab73-97d26b31abe0" "answer-ab51231a-c4b2-5897-b2e4-e89b02c56f79" "answer-923cac8c-34f5-5ea2-bb76-7bdba561dd9b" "answer-b141e339-58d5-542d-a915-4a9424c25f13" "answer-79e93ad4-10ea-55a5-b833-af0a652dd041"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11d7504b-2828-5ed4-ab73-97d26b31abe0" :answer/id #uuid "11d7504b-2828-5ed4-ab73-97d26b31abe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.34" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab51231a-c4b2-5897-b2e4-e89b02c56f79" :answer/id #uuid "ab51231a-c4b2-5897-b2e4-e89b02c56f79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.38" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-923cac8c-34f5-5ea2-bb76-7bdba561dd9b" :answer/id #uuid "923cac8c-34f5-5ea2-bb76-7bdba561dd9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b141e339-58d5-542d-a915-4a9424c25f13" :answer/id #uuid "b141e339-58d5-542d-a915-4a9424c25f13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.59" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79e93ad4-10ea-55a5-b833-af0a652dd041" :answer/id #uuid "79e93ad4-10ea-55a5-b833-af0a652dd041" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.62" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614" :question/id #uuid "bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614" :question/math-academy-id "q-85898" :question/problem "A professional soccer team participates in a championship. On average, the team has a $20%$ chance of losing each game. What is the probability that the team plays at least $4$ games without losing (i.e., the first loss occurs after the $4$th game)?" :question/worked-solution "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of games played until they lose their first game. Since the chance of losing each game is $20%,$ the number of games played before the team loses can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=20%=\\frac{20}{100}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.2).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.2)^{x-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{x-1}(0.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>4)=1-P(X\\le 4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{1-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{2-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{4-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.2)+(0.8)(0.2)+(0.8)^{2}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3}(0.2) \\\\  & =[1+0.8+(0.8)^{2}+(0.8)^{3}](0.2) \\\\  & =0.5904.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team will play at least $4$ games before their first loss\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.5904 \\\\  & =0.4096.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5a73d02-83b1-5576-b29f-c152341639c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5a73d02-83b1-5576-b29f-c152341639c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a73d02-83b1-5576-b29f-c152341639c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-92ca3ed3-4800-5b9b-9a1e-c2593faee6b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92ca3ed3-4800-5b9b-9a1e-c2593faee6b0" "answer-6bb6214c-874c-56eb-ac10-bd965a94ea7b" "answer-2c351f9d-e6dd-5545-9507-2a8c593f35e6" "answer-570a9d04-2464-5cc1-8a35-0e5260d61d27" "answer-09471886-d6ef-5fda-b2ee-571efe3d7fd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92ca3ed3-4800-5b9b-9a1e-c2593faee6b0" :answer/id #uuid "92ca3ed3-4800-5b9b-9a1e-c2593faee6b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4096" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6bb6214c-874c-56eb-ac10-bd965a94ea7b" :answer/id #uuid "6bb6214c-874c-56eb-ac10-bd965a94ea7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4795" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c351f9d-e6dd-5545-9507-2a8c593f35e6" :answer/id #uuid "2c351f9d-e6dd-5545-9507-2a8c593f35e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4980" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-570a9d04-2464-5cc1-8a35-0e5260d61d27" :answer/id #uuid "570a9d04-2464-5cc1-8a35-0e5260d61d27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09471886-d6ef-5fda-b2ee-571efe3d7fd3" :answer/id #uuid "09471886-d6ef-5fda-b2ee-571efe3d7fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5472" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-66c8dbdf-3c5b-559a-b97b-93f058b0fde3" :activity/id #uuid "66c8dbdf-3c5b-559a-b97b-93f058b0fde3" :activity/title "Modeling With the Geometric Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050305 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-689fdd30-f221-5416-a743-4ed6517268b3" "step-8eb6ad6a-8dea-571b-b04b-4da7ec78d1ae" "step-5dbd1cdf-7537-5f2a-b30a-6dc52b03a1a2" "step-d779f666-116f-5872-bbc1-9263cc0c06b4" "step-11a70fe3-9b0a-5634-8d0e-34e26561e654"] :activity/first-step "step-689fdd30-f221-5416-a743-4ed6517268b3" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-689fdd30-f221-5416-a743-4ed6517268b3" :step/id #uuid "689fdd30-f221-5416-a743-4ed6517268b3" :step/content 17592186157868 :step/next "step-8eb6ad6a-8dea-571b-b04b-4da7ec78d1ae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8eb6ad6a-8dea-571b-b04b-4da7ec78d1ae" :step/id #uuid "8eb6ad6a-8dea-571b-b04b-4da7ec78d1ae" :step/content "question-adc9ba22-54ae-50d6-b1c5-428a57977aea" :step/next "step-5dbd1cdf-7537-5f2a-b30a-6dc52b03a1a2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5dbd1cdf-7537-5f2a-b30a-6dc52b03a1a2" :step/id #uuid "5dbd1cdf-7537-5f2a-b30a-6dc52b03a1a2" :step/content "question-934447c8-2b56-5fbe-8d1c-94052a2dad02" :step/next "step-d779f666-116f-5872-bbc1-9263cc0c06b4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d779f666-116f-5872-bbc1-9263cc0c06b4" :step/id #uuid "d779f666-116f-5872-bbc1-9263cc0c06b4" :step/content "question-e49d61e7-549c-5568-9bc1-1e99e4e1790c" :step/next "step-11a70fe3-9b0a-5634-8d0e-34e26561e654" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-11a70fe3-9b0a-5634-8d0e-34e26561e654" :step/id #uuid "11a70fe3-9b0a-5634-8d0e-34e26561e654" :step/content "question-bc079e51-1277-52af-9686-51765572e614" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186205341 :answer-field/choices 17592186205344] [:db/retract 17592186205341 :answer-field/choices 17592186205345] [:db/retract 17592186205341 :answer-field/choices 17592186205346] [:db/retract 17592186205341 :answer-field/choices 17592186205342] [:db/retract 17592186205341 :answer-field/choices 17592186205343] {:db/id 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a" :question/id #uuid "8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-265760" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81f5b530-9e0f-51b8-82ba-e6d74b6b4686"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81f5b530-9e0f-51b8-82ba-e6d74b6b4686" :answer-field/id #uuid "81f5b530-9e0f-51b8-82ba-e6d74b6b4686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a4929fc1-3e70-5bff-a80f-2be44c2ad920" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70ee273e-1adf-5b0b-9c25-84a6f752229b" "answer-110945c0-e65b-5532-b050-4bd230c8fb1d" "answer-a4929fc1-3e70-5bff-a80f-2be44c2ad920" "answer-778742bb-372b-5ab3-9144-d2501865782a" "answer-1e5de0f5-f94e-561a-ac4c-228d4c9f8358"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ee273e-1adf-5b0b-9c25-84a6f752229b" :answer/id #uuid "70ee273e-1adf-5b0b-9c25-84a6f752229b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-110945c0-e65b-5532-b050-4bd230c8fb1d" :answer/id #uuid "110945c0-e65b-5532-b050-4bd230c8fb1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4929fc1-3e70-5bff-a80f-2be44c2ad920" :answer/id #uuid "a4929fc1-3e70-5bff-a80f-2be44c2ad920" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-778742bb-372b-5ab3-9144-d2501865782a" :answer/id #uuid "778742bb-372b-5ab3-9144-d2501865782a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e5de0f5-f94e-561a-ac4c-228d4c9f8358" :answer/id #uuid "1e5de0f5-f94e-561a-ac4c-228d4c9f8358" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208848 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208930 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)={y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205341 :answer-field/correct "answer-cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86d96929-a573-5ba1-a568-15337e2bcf56" "answer-cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" "answer-ff114911-6bea-5d83-a4a0-4820ac877a2b" "answer-e136544d-ec3c-563b-b13e-653feae14454" "answer-0288d1c8-90da-55c4-98e8-5a5eabefe8cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86d96929-a573-5ba1-a568-15337e2bcf56" :answer/id #uuid "86d96929-a573-5ba1-a568-15337e2bcf56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer/id #uuid "cc83261d-2254-5227-8497-098b5fbcaed7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -29\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff114911-6bea-5d83-a4a0-4820ac877a2b" :answer/id #uuid "ff114911-6bea-5d83-a4a0-4820ac877a2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e136544d-ec3c-563b-b13e-653feae14454" :answer/id #uuid "e136544d-ec3c-563b-b13e-653feae14454" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0288d1c8-90da-55c4-98e8-5a5eabefe8cc" :answer/id #uuid "0288d1c8-90da-55c4-98e8-5a5eabefe8cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :question/id #uuid "3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-269525" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" "answer-5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" "answer-263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" "answer-a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" "answer-ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" :answer/id #uuid "85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" :answer/id #uuid "5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" :answer/id #uuid "263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" :answer/id #uuid "a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186208847 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" "question-8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" :question/id #uuid "6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-276043" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+2 \\\\ 0-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c4950606-28b0-554b-95e7-9a39d66b5c16" "answer-field-5f8172f4-c9fe-5e8d-b6a6-b70f7a350883"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4950606-28b0-554b-95e7-9a39d66b5c16" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4950606-28b0-554b-95e7-9a39d66b5c16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-85a4f0d1-a936-5ab3-bd96-2206322e6e46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85a4f0d1-a936-5ab3-bd96-2206322e6e46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85a4f0d1-a936-5ab3-bd96-2206322e6e46" :answer/id #uuid "85a4f0d1-a936-5ab3-bd96-2206322e6e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5f8172f4-c9fe-5e8d-b6a6-b70f7a350883" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8172f4-c9fe-5e8d-b6a6-b70f7a350883" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-45292744-8b9c-5c5e-b27f-372a2cae9e71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45292744-8b9c-5c5e-b27f-372a2cae9e71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45292744-8b9c-5c5e-b27f-372a2cae9e71" :answer/id #uuid "45292744-8b9c-5c5e-b27f-372a2cae9e71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d" :question/id #uuid "8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-276044" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0-10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e69fbcb6-2b3e-5e04-95d1-2af8c246cb30" "answer-field-e1453ef1-7ffc-594f-a954-83c62ec2f697"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e69fbcb6-2b3e-5e04-95d1-2af8c246cb30" :answer-field/id #uuid "e69fbcb6-2b3e-5e04-95d1-2af8c246cb30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fbd51744-3817-5b56-83ea-b6c1a15c1145" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbd51744-3817-5b56-83ea-b6c1a15c1145"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd51744-3817-5b56-83ea-b6c1a15c1145" :answer/id #uuid "fbd51744-3817-5b56-83ea-b6c1a15c1145" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1453ef1-7ffc-594f-a954-83c62ec2f697" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1453ef1-7ffc-594f-a954-83c62ec2f697" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-abd7469e-6019-5f0b-8db1-f38b3d8887b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-abd7469e-6019-5f0b-8db1-f38b3d8887b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abd7469e-6019-5f0b-8db1-f38b3d8887b6" :answer/id #uuid "abd7469e-6019-5f0b-8db1-f38b3d8887b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-abde7dfb-d488-5e8b-96d4-bfad19b4670d" :activity/id #uuid "abde7dfb-d488-5e8b-96d4-bfad19b4670d" :activity/title "Further Vector Gradients" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050484 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-99b9d747-8358-5805-8669-518304d418d4" "step-6c50ec86-f31c-5ab7-bdc2-7da6231b1be3" "step-ac05d334-16c5-5e79-9fe0-8447dbb842a5" "step-3eaf924d-cd34-5820-a14e-44d3eca661aa" "step-44fdf552-67f4-506b-8352-bf3ff777ad37"] :activity/first-step "step-99b9d747-8358-5805-8669-518304d418d4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-99b9d747-8358-5805-8669-518304d418d4" :step/id #uuid "99b9d747-8358-5805-8669-518304d418d4" :step/content "question-8ea8eb91-918f-5aeb-b452-a90611b4dd2a" :step/next "step-6c50ec86-f31c-5ab7-bdc2-7da6231b1be3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6c50ec86-f31c-5ab7-bdc2-7da6231b1be3" :step/id #uuid "6c50ec86-f31c-5ab7-bdc2-7da6231b1be3" :step/content 17592186208930 :step/next "step-ac05d334-16c5-5e79-9fe0-8447dbb842a5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ac05d334-16c5-5e79-9fe0-8447dbb842a5" :step/id #uuid "ac05d334-16c5-5e79-9fe0-8447dbb842a5" :step/content "question-3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :step/next "step-3eaf924d-cd34-5820-a14e-44d3eca661aa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3eaf924d-cd34-5820-a14e-44d3eca661aa" :step/id #uuid "3eaf924d-cd34-5820-a14e-44d3eca661aa" :step/content "question-6d939167-bbdf-5642-a79d-3234f69c9c09" :step/next "step-44fdf552-67f4-506b-8352-bf3ff777ad37" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-44fdf552-67f4-506b-8352-bf3ff777ad37" :step/id #uuid "44fdf552-67f4-506b-8352-bf3ff777ad37" :step/content "question-8b961be3-a61d-5fd5-a33f-daa3b859082d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" "question-a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101467 :question/problem "Suppose that \n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n}{n!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{6}\\cdot 6}{6!}|=\\frac{6}{720}=\\frac{1}{120}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{120}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186099963 :answer-field/correct 17592186099965 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" "question-bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" :question/id #uuid "aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83612" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-1}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 2$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(i+1)^{2}-1}| & \\le 0.1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i+1-1} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ {i}^{2}+2i & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39823874-955e-5f49-9b30-1ee04d308856"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39823874-955e-5f49-9b30-1ee04d308856" :answer-field/id #uuid "39823874-955e-5f49-9b30-1ee04d308856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f10e5377-afe1-5456-b9e8-239ed8651fc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f10e5377-afe1-5456-b9e8-239ed8651fc7" "answer-1347e9be-27c7-561f-9660-bf0eb27ab9f0" "answer-8bc70d2a-9194-5400-ae1f-803748c50b7b" "answer-22838a7c-5f32-5a55-9a8a-210f19e97ab1" "answer-69d0a8d6-bd8f-5e35-9b5d-e6a76b35685c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f10e5377-afe1-5456-b9e8-239ed8651fc7" :answer/id #uuid "f10e5377-afe1-5456-b9e8-239ed8651fc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1347e9be-27c7-561f-9660-bf0eb27ab9f0" :answer/id #uuid "1347e9be-27c7-561f-9660-bf0eb27ab9f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bc70d2a-9194-5400-ae1f-803748c50b7b" :answer/id #uuid "8bc70d2a-9194-5400-ae1f-803748c50b7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22838a7c-5f32-5a55-9a8a-210f19e97ab1" :answer/id #uuid "22838a7c-5f32-5a55-9a8a-210f19e97ab1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{i}^{2}+4i\\ge 10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69d0a8d6-bd8f-5e35-9b5d-e6a76b35685c" :answer/id #uuid "69d0a8d6-bd8f-5e35-9b5d-e6a76b35685c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{i}^{2}+4i\\ge 98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb" :question/id #uuid "bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104031" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.01.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 1$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{{2}^{i+1}}| & \\le 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{i+1}} & \\le \\frac{1}{100} \\\\ 100 & \\le {2}^{i+1} \\\\ 100 & \\le 2\\cdot {2}^{i} \\\\ {2}^{i} & \\ge 50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94f7bcdd-1a29-50a3-b5ca-fac98fc4f9d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94f7bcdd-1a29-50a3-b5ca-fac98fc4f9d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "94f7bcdd-1a29-50a3-b5ca-fac98fc4f9d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0749bd24-a594-5cc9-83a7-36f58ac8d74e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4388f0fe-c065-54c2-b8d2-e04e3c15d306" "answer-390e483e-d568-5e74-bc98-5844f88422f6" "answer-0749bd24-a594-5cc9-83a7-36f58ac8d74e" "answer-8b29d420-50c8-516b-bbbf-e812563a039e" "answer-14c36eeb-9d6a-50b7-bad3-9d44cf5ab909"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4388f0fe-c065-54c2-b8d2-e04e3c15d306" :answer/id #uuid "4388f0fe-c065-54c2-b8d2-e04e3c15d306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-390e483e-d568-5e74-bc98-5844f88422f6" :answer/id #uuid "390e483e-d568-5e74-bc98-5844f88422f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0749bd24-a594-5cc9-83a7-36f58ac8d74e" :answer/id #uuid "0749bd24-a594-5cc9-83a7-36f58ac8d74e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b29d420-50c8-516b-bbbf-e812563a039e" :answer/id #uuid "8b29d420-50c8-516b-bbbf-e812563a039e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c36eeb-9d6a-50b7-bad3-9d44cf5ab909" :answer/id #uuid "14c36eeb-9d6a-50b7-bad3-9d44cf5ab909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" :question/id #uuid "26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352750" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$\n\nFind the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{n+2}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}}{{e}^{7+2}}|=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49c7b2b3-00cb-51d6-bf65-4c63b25afc6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49c7b2b3-00cb-51d6-bf65-4c63b25afc6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "49c7b2b3-00cb-51d6-bf65-4c63b25afc6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-42b4363e-f490-5a92-947a-8650b1bc2add" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42b4363e-f490-5a92-947a-8650b1bc2add"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42b4363e-f490-5a92-947a-8650b1bc2add" :answer/id #uuid "42b4363e-f490-5a92-947a-8650b1bc2add" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{9}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb" :question/id #uuid "a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-352755" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n}}{(n+1)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{{(-2)}^{6}}{(6+1)!}|=\\frac{64}{5040}=\\frac{4}{315}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{4}{315}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aafd742f-ff73-5b71-bc91-fdad1d0edbed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aafd742f-ff73-5b71-bc91-fdad1d0edbed" :answer-field/id #uuid "aafd742f-ff73-5b71-bc91-fdad1d0edbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e740dac2-bdf9-576f-b582-e4da8c53bb96" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e740dac2-bdf9-576f-b582-e4da8c53bb96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e740dac2-bdf9-576f-b582-e4da8c53bb96" :answer/id #uuid "e740dac2-bdf9-576f-b582-e4da8c53bb96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{315}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2a564e61-70d4-550a-89c2-4db5bd9ae08b" :activity/id #uuid "2a564e61-70d4-550a-89c2-4db5bd9ae08b" :activity/title "The Alternating Series Error Bound" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050912 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-e78a6e2d-2359-5c60-b93c-2d7ced996f4e" "step-82473a92-9dc4-5f79-bf3f-843f63c36ff6" "step-3d748b43-7f4a-55a5-b9f2-b2b1e447b3e8" "step-52608748-4925-5f07-89c8-df6f306d09b8" "step-e0477d66-d0da-57fe-ae64-7a88c2a0d0bc"] :activity/first-step "step-e78a6e2d-2359-5c60-b93c-2d7ced996f4e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e78a6e2d-2359-5c60-b93c-2d7ced996f4e" :step/id #uuid "e78a6e2d-2359-5c60-b93c-2d7ced996f4e" :step/content 17592186101467 :step/next "step-82473a92-9dc4-5f79-bf3f-843f63c36ff6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-82473a92-9dc4-5f79-bf3f-843f63c36ff6" :step/id #uuid "82473a92-9dc4-5f79-bf3f-843f63c36ff6" :step/content "question-aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" :step/next "step-3d748b43-7f4a-55a5-b9f2-b2b1e447b3e8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d748b43-7f4a-55a5-b9f2-b2b1e447b3e8" :step/id #uuid "3d748b43-7f4a-55a5-b9f2-b2b1e447b3e8" :step/content "question-bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb" :step/next "step-52608748-4925-5f07-89c8-df6f306d09b8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-52608748-4925-5f07-89c8-df6f306d09b8" :step/id #uuid "52608748-4925-5f07-89c8-df6f306d09b8" :step/content "question-26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" :step/next "step-e0477d66-d0da-57fe-ae64-7a88c2a0d0bc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e0477d66-d0da-57fe-ae64-7a88c2a0d0bc" :step/id #uuid "e0477d66-d0da-57fe-ae64-7a88c2a0d0bc" :step/content "question-a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186107941 :answer-field/choices 17592186107942] [:db/retract 17592186107941 :answer-field/choices 17592186107943] [:db/retract 17592186107941 :answer-field/choices 17592186107944] [:db/retract 17592186107941 :answer-field/choices 17592186107945] [:db/retract 17592186107941 :answer-field/choices 17592186107946] {:db/id 17592186110696 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a84d4a4-1873-5e3e-810d-d5ca7ce87d53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110486 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡2+{log}_{3}⁡5 & ={log}_{3}⁡(2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110697 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c7c12aa8-de03-59c9-ab5d-5e64e226cea3" "question-36bfe507-bf36-5e20-b82e-5099f50d12ef" "question-9ea8b2b1-a8ee-55af-9a04-8ea83c2d0aaa" "question-f62edb2c-be7a-51bc-a4d3-e51bf196542b" "question-56e99923-e71e-5783-b293-6f220455cbaa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110487 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡7+3{log}_{5}⁡3 & =3({log}_{5}⁡7+{log}_{5}⁡3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3{log}_{5}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log_{5}{7}+3\\log_{5}{3} = 3\\log_{5} 21 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110698 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0be7ea7e-f479-5c43-ae90-b193d0f73efd" "question-fbe8f3d1-3c93-5a68-ac60-38c193b6c6b9" "question-c529b9a8-1cea-5cb5-8b9e-8f76c8202de5" "question-fba4c35a-120c-5bd8-b872-2cfc6815a465" "question-64aa673c-cb55-5490-a5e1-8f5f8d78ed0e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110488 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln{a} + \\ln{b} = \\ln(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the given expression can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110699 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35a5df48-5462-5c58-bbc0-60fbb99c3f4a" "question-4ea44857-e35f-51f3-a0fe-716b02fb3232"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110489 :question/problem "If $\\log_2(3)=k$ and $\\log_2(x)=p,$ what is the value of $\\log_2(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\log_n a + \\log_n b = \\log_n(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{2}⁡3+{log}_{2}⁡x \\\\  & =k+p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111031 :question/problem "Express ${log}_{5}⁡2+{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡6$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡2+{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡6 & ={log}_{5}⁡(2\\cdot 4)+{log}_{5}⁡6 \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡8+{log}_{5}⁡6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡8+{log}_{5}⁡6 & ={log}_{5}⁡(8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107941 :answer-field/correct "answer-8d50a5b0-9844-5e2c-97e0-9c0453654ffa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15010333-4fe3-58c3-9b8d-774f22c98a0f" "answer-566a2d36-e8a0-5180-945f-a8d73a39229d" "answer-44f711d5-f916-58a0-9fd2-f9692427848c" "answer-34191607-a84f-520a-941b-c61abefe22a0" "answer-8d50a5b0-9844-5e2c-97e0-9c0453654ffa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15010333-4fe3-58c3-9b8d-774f22c98a0f" :answer/id #uuid "15010333-4fe3-58c3-9b8d-774f22c98a0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{125}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566a2d36-e8a0-5180-945f-a8d73a39229d" :answer/id #uuid "566a2d36-e8a0-5180-945f-a8d73a39229d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{15}⁡12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44f711d5-f916-58a0-9fd2-f9692427848c" :answer/id #uuid "44f711d5-f916-58a0-9fd2-f9692427848c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{15}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34191607-a84f-520a-941b-c61abefe22a0" :answer/id #uuid "34191607-a84f-520a-941b-c61abefe22a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d50a5b0-9844-5e2c-97e0-9c0453654ffa" :answer/id #uuid "8d50a5b0-9844-5e2c-97e0-9c0453654ffa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{5}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a84d4a4-1873-5e3e-810d-d5ca7ce87d53" :question/id #uuid "3a84d4a4-1873-5e3e-810d-d5ca7ce87d53" :question/math-academy-id "q-66743" :question/problem "Express ${log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡8+{log}_{6}⁡3+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(8\\cdot 3)+{log}_{6}⁡2 \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule for logarithms once more, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡24+{log}_{6}⁡2 & ={log}_{6}⁡(24\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ={log}_{6}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bffb35e1-1e25-5cd4-871a-eb15db9f4c0d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bffb35e1-1e25-5cd4-871a-eb15db9f4c0d" :answer-field/id #uuid "bffb35e1-1e25-5cd4-871a-eb15db9f4c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a3635b55-e19b-56d9-8c0a-0e8f74d3c21f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6563490e-1ac0-53ef-ab99-05500d25141e" "answer-f33b67b7-0bd9-5cf2-97c4-bb4c7e0b9a78" "answer-e3d4e277-0fc4-529c-ada0-4c7bf18a7cf2" "answer-7e056c0f-0002-5d5b-bf81-a67b2f76e5c4" "answer-a3635b55-e19b-56d9-8c0a-0e8f74d3c21f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6563490e-1ac0-53ef-ab99-05500d25141e" :answer/id #uuid "6563490e-1ac0-53ef-ab99-05500d25141e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{18}⁡13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f33b67b7-0bd9-5cf2-97c4-bb4c7e0b9a78" :answer/id #uuid "f33b67b7-0bd9-5cf2-97c4-bb4c7e0b9a78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{18}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3d4e277-0fc4-529c-ada0-4c7bf18a7cf2" :answer/id #uuid "e3d4e277-0fc4-529c-ada0-4c7bf18a7cf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{216}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e056c0f-0002-5d5b-bf81-a67b2f76e5c4" :answer/id #uuid "7e056c0f-0002-5d5b-bf81-a67b2f76e5c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{6}⁡13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3635b55-e19b-56d9-8c0a-0e8f74d3c21f" :answer/id #uuid "a3635b55-e19b-56d9-8c0a-0e8f74d3c21f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{log}_{6}⁡48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c7c12aa8-de03-59c9-ab5d-5e64e226cea3" :question/id #uuid "c7c12aa8-de03-59c9-ab5d-5e64e226cea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-250764" :question/problem "Write the following expression as a single logarithm.\n\n$5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡p+\\operatorname{ln}⁡q+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(pq)) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p\\cdot q\\cdot pq) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({p}^{2}{q}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f88f9804-ffdf-554f-8671-c239c2d469a6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f88f9804-ffdf-554f-8671-c239c2d469a6" :answer-field/id #uuid "f88f9804-ffdf-554f-8671-c239c2d469a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d762dc30-ad94-5117-9809-03ee4cc6d7fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d762dc30-ad94-5117-9809-03ee4cc6d7fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d762dc30-ad94-5117-9809-03ee4cc6d7fc" :answer/id #uuid "d762dc30-ad94-5117-9809-03ee4cc6d7fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p^2q^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-36bfe507-bf36-5e20-b82e-5099f50d12ef" :question/id #uuid "36bfe507-bf36-5e20-b82e-5099f50d12ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-99909" :question/problem "Express $8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡a+{log}_{n}⁡b={log}_{n}⁡(ab)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the product rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & =8(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡21.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca912710-1e96-5c92-97a7-f13c2675599d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca9127